  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing GBV services in Debremarkos Town, Amhara Region through community awareness raising, capacity building  strengthening One-Stop-Center</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">ltpgtGender-based violence (GBV) particularly against women is unfortunately common during armed conflicts. No rigorous and comprehensive empirical work has documented the extent of GBV and its consequences that took place during the war. This project aims to address GBV within the conflict-affected regions of Amhara. While the Tigray-Ethiopian government conflicts concluded on November 2, 2022, as per the Pretoria agreement, another conflict has arisen in the Amhara Region involving the Amhara Fano and Ethiopian government forces. Consequently, this situation has compromised GBV services, posing a significant risk to the well-being of women and girls.lt/pgtltpgtltbrgtSo that, the project seeks to enhance GBV services in Debre Markos town, Amhara region by mobilizing the community through well-framed methodologies. Additionally, the project will strengthen and enhance GBV services by straightening One-Stop-Center (OSC) . It will also build the capacity of various stakeholders on GBV prevention and response mechanisms. To provide support to GBV survivors, the project will train and deploy peer counselors who will offer case management services. Furthermore, a functional referral pathway will be established to ensure the comprehensive provision of GBV services.lt/pgtltpgt&#xA0; ltbrgtThe project planned to raise awareness among a total of 28,707 targeted individuals through various modalities. Additionally, a structured peer learning program will reach 1,800 women and girls. Moreover, 70 GBV survivors will receive cash or in-kind support based on individual safety plans that will be preparedltbrgtlt/pgt</narrative>
  </description>
